# ============================================================================
# SMTP App - Docker build context exclusions
#
# HOW THIS FILE IS SELECTED
#   Docker automatically pairs a Dockerfile with an ignore file named after
#   it in the same directory: apps/smtp/Dockerfile -> apps/smtp/
#   Dockerfile.dockerignore. When this file exists, it REPLACES the
#   repository-root .dockerignore for this build - the two are NOT merged -
#   so this file must be self-contained. (Docker 25+; "Dockerfile-specific
#   .dockerignore" in the Docker docs.)
#
# PATH SEMANTICS
#   The build context is the MONOREPO ROOT, two levels above this file (the
#   app uses pnpm workspace:*/catalog: dependencies that only resolve from
#   the root - see the Dockerfile header and compose.yml). All patterns
#   below are therefore relative to the repository root, NOT to apps/smtp/
#   - even though this file lives in that directory.
#
# WHY PER-APP FILES (instead of only the shared root .dockerignore)
#   - Each app's build carries its ignore rules right next to its
#     Dockerfile, so the two are versioned, reviewed and changed together.
#   - The two apps can diverge (different env files, auth files, artifacts)
#     without touching a shared file that other builds rely on.
#   - "Exclude every other app, keep this one" cannot be expressed from a
#     single shared root file at all - a root file does not know which
#     Dockerfile is being built.
#
# GOAL
#   Keep the context small and focused on this app (this app's sources, the
#   shared packages/ workspace packages, and the root workspace files), and
#   most importantly keep secrets and local state OUT of the context so
#   they can never end up in image layers.
#
# ORDERING CONSTRAINT - DO NOT REORDER CARELESSLY
#   Matching follows gitignore rules: the LAST matching pattern wins. The
#   "keep this app" negation below therefore MUST come BEFORE the artifact
#   and secret exclusions. If it came after, it would re-include this
#   app's node_modules/, .next/, .env* and .saleor-app-auth*.json files
#   (they are direct children of the app directory and match !apps/smtp/*),
#   silently defeating every exclusion above.
#   Verified empirically: with the negation last, the app's .next,
#   node_modules and env files leak into the context; with the negation
#   first they are excluded again.
# ============================================================================

# --- The other apps: not needed by this build (MUST stay at the top) ------
# Exclude the contents of every apps/<app>/ directory, then re-include this
# app's contents. Two-level form (exclude children, then re-include) is
# deliberate: gitignore-style matching cannot re-include a path whose
# parent directory was excluded, so we exclude at the *children* level,
# where the negation can win.
apps/*/*
!apps/smtp/*

# --- VCS / agent-tooling directories: not needed by the build -------------
.git
.cursor
.claude

# --- Dependencies and build artifacts (rebuilt inside the image) ----------
# These also re-exclude this app's own node_modules/ and .next/, which the
# negation above would otherwise let through (see ordering constraint).
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/.turbo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# --- Secrets and local state: NEVER baked into images ----------------------
# .env.docker is the gitignored runtime env (bind-mounted via compose);
# .env.local holds local dev values (including SECRET_KEY). The build
# receives every value it needs via ARG/ENV in the Dockerfile, so no env
# file is needed in the context at all.
**/.env
**/.env.*
# The app authentication file (FileAPL) and any local copies of it. It is
# mounted into the running container by compose; it must never be built.
**/.saleor-app-auth*.json

# --- Local logs and OS noise ----------------------------------------------
**/*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# CAUTION: BuildKit's context cache key does NOT include this file's
# content. After changing it, a plain build can silently reuse cached
# layers from the OLD context. Rebuild with
#   docker compose build --no-cache
# and verify the pure image (docker create + docker export on the
# image, NOT the running container - the FileAPL bind mount hides the
# image file; see the README, Validating the container).
